### Mandatory Repomix Package (External Review)

- Package: `.agents/repomix_packages/repomix-pr-0228-workspace-contract-review.xml`
- Template: `code-review`
- Included files: `27`

### Goal Shape To Review Against

At the named `laptop` and `desktop` review widths, Klassrumskartan should behave like a bounded
workspace frame rather than ordinary page content:

1. Page header, planner top panel, and toolbar remain stable chrome above the active workspace.
2. The active workspace body becomes one bounded region that owns the visible grouping/seating work
   area.
3. Inside that region, the student-pool rail is a stable side lane with a fixed visible header and
   a scrolling list body.
4. The active right-hand lane owns the deeper grouping-board or seating-canvas scrolling.
5. The toolbar and workspace body must not disappear, collapse, or change overflow behavior merely
   because the browser crosses a breakpoint or is resized through an intermediate width band.
6. Smaller breakpoints may intentionally diverge from the desktop-heavy workspace model, but the
   cutover must be explicit and stable rather than accidental or shell-fragile.

### Review Resolution

`PR-0228` is on a materially better implementation baseline than the earlier rejected bounded-pane
pass, but it is not ready to close yet.

The previously rejected runtime pane-height seam is now gone from the shared shell path:
`PlannerWorkspaceModeSurface.vue` is back to a thin shared wrapper, planner geometry is named in
`plannerWorkspaceLayout.ts` and authored in shared CSS primitives, and the retained Playwright lane
now proves the authenticated real-data shell at `1279x900`, `1366x768`, and `1440x900` using
`SA24D` / `G20`.

However, the slice still stops short of full close-out against the written docs/review contract:

- `PR-0228` still described parts of the older bounded-pane/right-lane-scroll model until this
  reconciliation pass, so the backlog trail needed to be aligned with the corrected page/workspace
  scroll implementation before any closure decision.
- The current retained browser proof is now auth-only and real-data-based, which is a healthier
  baseline than toy fixtures, but the written PR contract still expects guest/authenticated parity
  before the slice can move to `done`.
- `ST-29-11` and `EPIC-29` needed to acknowledge that `PR-0228` has improved the local shell
  baseline without yet satisfying the remaining retained review/proof obligations.

### Structural Fault Lines

1. `docs/backlog/prs/pr-0228-st-29-11-follow-up-desktop-student-pool-rail-stickiness-restoration.md`
   carried forward parts of the rejected bounded-pane/right-lane internal-scroll model even after
   the local implementation moved back to page/workspace scroll first. That mismatch made the slice
   look less complete on paper than it now is in code.
2. `scripts/playwright_pr_0227_group_board_height_contract_check.py` now proves the corrected
   authenticated shell on real `SA24D` / `G20` data, including the `1279px` band, but it no longer
   provides retained guest-route proof. That means guest/auth parity is currently carried by shared
   components and focused specs rather than a fresh live browser pass.
3. `docs/backlog/stories/story-29-11-klassrumskartan-shared-site-and-app-dense-control-primitive-tightening.md`
   and `docs/backlog/epics/epic-29-klassrumskartan-desktop-first-workspace-overhaul.md` needed an
   explicit in-progress note so the docs trail recognizes the stronger local baseline without
   accidentally implying that `PR-0228` is already fully closed.

### Disproven Assumptions

- The current implementation is **not** still on the rejected runtime-measured pane-shell model.
  The shared shell seam is now CSS-owned again and the page/workspace scroll path is back.
- The remaining blocker is **not** primarily shell geometry authorship anymore. The stronger local
  baseline now exists; the gap is closure against the retained docs/review/proof expectations.
- The current retained proof lane is **stronger** than the earlier rejected version because it uses
  real `SA24D` / `G20` data and covers the `1279px` resize band, but it is still **narrower** than
  the written guest/auth parity expectation for final close-out.

### Fix Directions

1. Keep the corrected CSS-owned page/workspace scroll model as the baseline and align the backlog
   contract to it.
   Pros: preserves the healthier shell implementation already in place and removes stale task/review
   language that still points at the rejected pane model.
   Cons: requires touching the retained review/task/story trail before the slice can be called
   done.
2. Make an explicit close-out decision on guest/auth parity.
   Pros: either the slice gains the missing retained guest browser proof, or the docs explicitly
   narrow the live-proof obligation while keeping guest parity enforced through shared-shell specs.
   Cons: this is still additional work; the current auth-only proof is not enough if the broader
   parity expectation remains unchanged.
3. Only after those two steps, decide whether `PR-0228` can move to `done` or should remain a
   narrower hardening follow-up.
   Pros: keeps the review honest and avoids claiming closure while the retained contract is still
   split between old wording and newer implementation.
   Cons: delays formal close-out even though the implementation baseline is already much healthier.

### Decision Approvals

- [x] `PR-0228` remains a valid bounded `ST-29-11` follow-up rather than a full workspace-redesign
      restart.
- [x] The current CSS-owned page/workspace scroll implementation is a healthier baseline than the
      previously rejected bounded-pane model.
- [ ] The retained docs/review trail is now aligned enough to mark `PR-0228` done without another
      closure decision.
- [ ] The current auth-only real-data proof is sufficient to satisfy the written guest/auth parity
      expectation.
- [ ] `PR-0228` may move to `done` today.

## Changes Made

1. `PR-0228` now explicitly documents the corrected CSS-owned page/workspace scroll baseline.
2. `ST-29-11` and `EPIC-29` now record that `PR-0228` is improved locally but not yet formally
   closed.
3. The retained review record remains open until the final guest/auth parity close-out decision is
